C# INTERVIEW – SHORT ANSWERS WITH EXAMPLES
1️⃣ Difference between .NET and C#

.NET is a platform/runtime.
C# is a programming language that runs on .NET.

// C# code
Console.WriteLine("Runs on .NET");

2️⃣ IL (Intermediate Language)

IL is CPU-independent code generated by the compiler.

Flow:
C# → IL → Native code (via JIT)

3️⃣ JIT (Just-In-Time Compiler)

Converts IL code to machine code at runtime.

Benefit: Platform independence + optimization.

4️⃣ CLR (Common Language Runtime)

Manages:

Memory

Garbage Collection

Exception handling

Security

5️⃣ Managed vs Unmanaged Code
Managed	Unmanaged
Runs under CLR	Runs outside CLR
Auto GC	Manual memory
Safe	Risky

Example:

// Managed
string name = "C#";

6️⃣ Stack vs Heap
Stack	Heap
Value types	Reference types
Faster	Slower
Auto cleanup	GC managed
7️⃣ Value vs Reference Types
int a = 10;        // Value type
string s = "Hi";  // Reference type

8️⃣ Boxing & Unboxing
int x = 10;
object o = x;      // Boxing
int y = (int)o;    // Unboxing


⚠️ Performance cost

9️⃣ Array vs ArrayList
Array	ArrayList
Fixed size	Dynamic
Type-safe	Not type-safe
Faster	Slower
🔟 Generics

Provides type safety and performance.

List<int> list = new List<int>();

1️⃣1️⃣ Thread

A lightweight unit of execution.

Thread t = new Thread(Method);
t.Start();

1️⃣2️⃣ Thread vs TPL
Thread	TPL
Manual	Automatic
Complex	Easy
Low level	High level
Task.Run(() => Console.WriteLine("TPL"));

1️⃣3️⃣ Exception Handling
try { }
catch(Exception ex) { }
finally { }


finally always executes.

1️⃣4️⃣ out Keyword

Used to return multiple values.

void Get(out int x)
{
    x = 10;
}

1️⃣5️⃣ Delegate

A type-safe function pointer.

delegate void MyDel();
MyDel d = Method;

1️⃣6️⃣ Multicast Delegate

Points to multiple methods.

d += Method2;

1️⃣7️⃣ Event

Used for publisher–subscriber pattern.

public event Action MyEvent;

1️⃣8️⃣ OOP Pillars

Encapsulation

Abstraction

Inheritance

Polymorphism

1️⃣9️⃣ Encapsulation

Wrapping data + behavior.

class A
{
    private int x;
}

2️⃣0️⃣ Abstraction

Hiding implementation.

abstract class Shape
{
    public abstract void Draw();
}

2️⃣1️⃣ Inheritance
class Child : Parent { }

2️⃣2️⃣ Polymorphism

Same method, different behavior.

class A { public virtual void Show(){} }
class B : A { public override void Show(){} }

2️⃣3️⃣ Method Overloading

Same method name, different parameters.

int Add(int a, int b)
double Add(double a, double b)

2️⃣4️⃣ Overloading vs Overriding
Overloading	Overriding
Compile time	Runtime
Same class	Parent-child
Different params	Same signature
2️⃣5️⃣ Abstract Class

Can have implementation

Cannot be instantiated

abstract class A
{
    public abstract void Test();
}

2️⃣6️⃣ Interface

Only contracts

Multiple inheritance allowed

interface ITest
{
    void Run();
}

2️⃣7️⃣ Abstract vs Interface
Abstract	Interface
Can have fields	No fields
Single inheritance	Multiple
Constructors allowed	Not allowed
2️⃣8️⃣ Constructor

Used to initialize objects.

class A
{
    public A() { }
}

2️⃣9️⃣ Constructor Order

👉 Parent constructor executes first

3️⃣0️⃣ Static Constructor

Executes once

Before first use

static A() { }

3️⃣1️⃣ Method Hiding (Shadowing)
class B : A
{
    public new void Show() { }
}

3️⃣2️⃣ Sealed Class

Cannot be inherited.

sealed class Final { }

3️⃣3️⃣ Partial Class

Split class into multiple files.

partial class Test { }
